{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789672821,"nonce":"Nd9eTzk6fpKjMgu5NNYR0I8WYQ0=","message":"Further nbody_modified evidence from the post-opt HIR dump: nbody_modified takes f64, i64, and two %List{ i64, i64, i64 } aggregate parameters. HirInterpreter::jit_dispatch_supported rejects struct/aggregate parameters, so even after the global-storage and typed-extern fixes, this hot function cannot dispatch through the current interpreter-to-native bridge. The Python HIR also contains indirect calls in reachable support code. Interpreter loop OSR is still absent, so a once-entered advance loop would remain interpreted. This is why a TieredRuntime entry-point switch alone cannot preserve the kernel's behavior and speed; the aggregate ABI bridge, interpreter back-edge transfer, and native-to-cold-call path must be implemented together.","files":null}]}